# ADR-018: Move verification rules to core (Shared Kernel)

## Status

Accepted

## Context

After the Tool-Lifecycle refactor (ADR-017), every concrete tool
implements its own verify_before / verify_after by calling the pure
check_xxx functions in app.verification.rules. As a result, tools
from intake, approval, and booking all import from the verification
bounded context. This violates the cross-context independence rule
established in ADR-007 and surfaced as a failure of the
test_bounded_contexts_do_not_import_from_each_other fitness function.

The verification rules themselves are not specific to the verification
bounded context. They are pure domain functions: no I/O, no state, no
context-specific knowledge. Every bounded context consumes them. They
are a Shared Kernel concept (DDD), and the project already has a home
for that: app.core.

## Decision

Move src/app/verification/rules.py to src/app/core/verification_rules.py
and update every importer accordingly. The check_xxx function names and
signatures stay unchanged.

The verification bounded context retains POTool and BudgetTool. It is
no longer the owner of the rule library; it is now just another consumer.

## Consequences

Positive: cross-context independence (ADR-007) is restored. The rule
library lives where its dependency shape says it should: in the Shared
Kernel, depended on by every context, depending on no context. The
fitness function passes. Adding a new bounded context that needs to
call verification rules no longer requires importing from another
context.

Negative: the verification/ directory's name is now slightly misleading
- it still hosts the two verification-phase tools (PO, Budget) but no
longer the rules themselves. The directory is kept under its current
name because renaming a bounded context is a larger concern with its
own ADR.

The architecture fitness function test_verification_rules_module_is_pure
was updated to point at the new path; this is a test-side path edit, not
a semantic change.

## Alternatives considered

Special-casing the fitness function to allow imports from
app.verification.rules across contexts was considered. Rejected because
it would weaken the cross-context independence principle for the sake
of preserving a file location. The principle is more valuable than the
location.

Duplicating the rule functions per bounded context was considered.
Rejected as it would introduce drift risk for what are intentionally
single-source-of-truth domain rules (ADR-004).
